It is leprechauns who get ROBBED, not the ones who rob: it is them who
hold the gold pot at the end of the Rainbow and you who want it. But
in Nethack you have to kill the leprechaun to recover the gold it
snatched! And engage in cannibally (l is humanoid) to acquire
teleportitis (a metaphor for airplanes?).

The correct thing would be then to hit a leprechaun to make it DROP
SOME GOLD. After you hit the leprechaun and he drops some gold, he
teleport-jumps. Dropped gold would be a fraction of its inventory,
just like currently but inversely, so you would have an incentive to
chase the leprechaun, to get more gold, just like now you do to
recover your gold. It would also make sense to create them scared,
when not asleep, so they would be fleeing you. A leprechaun without
gold would just teleport if hit... and hide in any available vault!
Maybe this was what was meant by the original programmer when vaults
were introduced?

If I remember well, it was goblins, hobgoblins and/or brownies the
ones who steal gold in classical tales, not leprechauns. I think this
mechanic is the correct one because the classical tale does have a
substrate and leprechaun is not a foreign type among Humans, so the
game is actually imposing an image of thief and thievery against the
image of satisfaction the classical tale imbues. Eating a leprechaun
to acquire teleportitis should be considered as cannibal behaviour and
treated thus according to your alignment.

And the metaphor can extend...

